# Option, Result, `?` and serde

## Absence & failure as types

```rust
enum Option<T> { Some(T), None }
enum Result<T, E> { Ok(T), Err(E) }
```

No null, no silent exceptions — the type signature tells you what can fail.

## The `?` operator

```rust
fn read_config() -> Result<Config, ConfigError> {
    let text = std::fs::read_to_string("app.json")?;   // early-return on Err
    let cfg = serde_json::from_str(&text)?;
    Ok(cfg)
}
```

`?` unwraps success or returns the error from the function. It composes;
nested match pyramids disappear.

## serde: one derive, many formats

```rust
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
struct Config {
    server_port: u16,
}
```

```rust
let json = serde_json::to_string_pretty(&config)?;
let back: Config = serde_json::from_str(&json)?;
```

This exact pattern powers this app's IPC boundary — Rust structs ↔ JSON.

## When panic IS right

Truly unrecoverable invariants (`assert!` on internal logic) may panic. For
everything user- or environment-shaped: return `Result`.

